<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<resources>
    <string name="health_screen_title">Password health</string>
    <string name="health_screen_subtitle">Offline analysis · nothing is sent anywhere</string>

    <string name="health_category_reused_label">Reused passwords</string>
    <string name="health_category_reused_note">One breach exposes every account sharing it</string>

    <!-- Was "Under 60 bits of entropy" — a threshold nothing in the app computes for stored
         passwords. The detector is StrengthAnalyzer.isWeak, i.e. a zxcvbn 0-4 guessability score
         of 1 or less, which its own KDoc calls "guessed in seconds" (roughly 20 bits, not 60).
         Entropy is computed only in the generator, never for stored items; describe what is
         actually measured. -->
    <string name="health_category_weak_label">Weak passwords</string>
    <string name="health_category_weak_note">Guessed in seconds by a common cracker</string>

    <string name="health_category_breached_label">Potentially compromised</string>
    <string name="health_category_breached_note">Matched the offline breach set</string>

    <string name="health_category_old_label">Old passwords</string>
    <string name="health_category_old_note">Unchanged for 90+ days</string>
    <!-- Dialog title for the "old passwords" category — includes the threshold, unlike the row label. -->
    <string name="health_category_old_dialog_title">Old passwords (90+ days)</string>

    <string name="health_category_duplicate_label">Duplicate entries</string>
    <string name="health_category_duplicate_note">The same account saved more than once</string>

    <!-- Accessibility label combining a category row's label, count, and note into one sentence. -->
    <string name="health_category_accessibility">%1$s: %2$d. %3$s</string>

    <!-- footnote shown when the offline breach corpus loaded
         successfully. Discloses the false-positive rate per spec §12.7: about 1 in 1,000
         passwords that are NOT on the list are flagged anyway.
         %1$s is the corpus size, already locale-formatted and compacted ("9.9M") by
         BreachCorpusFootnote.formatCorpusSize.

         There were two variants of this until 2026-08-10, and the dated one led with
         ", from July 2026". Both the count and the date were false precision, and the date was the
         worse of the two because it decays: the corpus is a static bundled asset, so a user
         installing next spring reads the month as a neglected feed rather than as a top-10M list
         whose value barely changes with age. The date is not lost — Settings → Breach list shows
         it, which is where someone asking about provenance actually goes.

         The clause from "A miss means" to "guarantee of safety" is byte-identical to what shipped
         before, in all five locales. That is deliberate: it is a security-property claim, and
         rewriting the clause in front of it should not reopen the verification protocol behind it.

         The hit clause was NOT identical, and had to change on 2026-08-31. It read "A hit is right
         about 999 times in 1,000", and 0.999 is 1 minus the disclosed false-positive rate
         (BreachFilter.DISCLOSED_FALSE_POSITIVE_DENOMINATOR, 1-in-2^riceK, 1-in-1024 at the shipped
         k=10). That complement is the specificity, P(no hit | not in the set) — the sentence
         attached it to the converse, P(in the set | hit), which is precision and depends on how
         many of THIS user's passwords are actually breached. Solving for 0.999 needs about half of
         them to be; for a vault of generated passwords every hit that ever appears is a false one.
         So the comment above claiming the line "discloses the false-positive rate" was itself
         wrong — it disclosed the complement, in the wrong direction. The line now states the rate
         the filter actually guarantees, and the key is in the Tier G register so the inverted form
         cannot come back in any of the five languages.

         Known coupling, not fixed here: 1,000 is written into the sentence in all five locales
         while the real rate is 2^-riceK read from the loaded asset, so a corpus rebuilt with a
         different k would make this line quietly wrong. Formatting it from
         DISCLOSED_FALSE_POSITIVE_DENOMINATOR needs a second format argument at the call site. -->
    <!-- SECURITY-COPY: tier G -->
    <string name="health_footnote_breach_available_undated">Checked against %1$s known-breached passwords. A miss means “not in the most-common-breached set” — never a guarantee of safety. A hit is not proof either: about 1 in 1,000 passwords that are not on the list get flagged anyway; the check trades that for keeping the whole list on your device.</string>
    <!-- Footnote shown when the offline breach corpus failed to load or parse. -->
    <string name="health_footnote_breach_unavailable">The breach list could not be read on this device, so nothing here can tell you whether a password has already leaked. Everything else on this screen was checked normally.</string>
    <!-- Footnote shown when the offline breach corpus loaded fine but nothing was actually looked
         up in it this scan: an empty vault, or a vault whose Logins carry no password (routine
         after a browser import that drops passwords). Deliberately NOT the "checked against"
         wording above — no check ran, so this states what the corpus is rather than claiming it
         was consulted. %1$s is the same locale-formatted corpus size. Not Tier G: it makes no
         security-property claim, it explicitly says nothing has been checked yet. -->
    <string name="health_footnote_breach_not_yet_checked">%1$s known-breached passwords are stored on this device, but nothing here has a password to check yet.</string>

    <!-- Body of the category detail dialog when no items match the selected category. -->
    <string name="health_dialog_empty_category">Nothing in this category.</string>
    <!-- Body of the category detail dialog when the search field narrows the list to zero rows.
         Distinct from health_dialog_empty_category: the category is not empty, the query just
         missed everything in it. -->
    <string name="health_dialog_no_matches">No matches.</string>
    <!-- Placeholder for the search field shown above a long category detail list. -->
    <string name="health_dialog_search_placeholder">Search this list</string>
    <string name="health_dialog_close">Close</string>
    <!-- Fallback title for a vault item with a blank title, in the category detail list. -->
    <string name="health_item_untitled">Untitled</string>
    <!-- Reused-passwords drill-down row, secondary line (F-30): how many OTHER items share this
         row's password, not counting itself. %1$d is sharedWithOthersCount, substituted only in
         the "other" form. Shown next to (or instead of, when the Login has no username) the
         row's username, since two reused Logins routinely share a title with nothing else on the
         row to tell them apart. -->
    <plurals name="health_dialog_reused_shared_with">
        <item quantity="one">Shares a password with 1 other account</item>
        <item quantity="other">Shares a password with %1$d other accounts</item>
    </plurals>

    <!-- Shown inside the score ring in place of a numeral when nothing has been scanned yet. An
         empty vault used to read "100", a perfect score for having done nothing. A glyph, not
         language, so it is never translated. -->
    <string name="health_score_placeholder" translatable="false">—</string>
    <!-- Small caption under the numeral inside the score ring. Kept short and shouty by design. -->
    <string name="health_score_badge">SCORE</string>
    <!-- Accessibility label for the score ring; %1$d is the score out of 100. -->
    <!-- 2026-09-13: was "Security score", and the glossary rules that word out here: security
         names a category (the drawer group and the Settings group), both of which contain password
         health, and this screen's own title is "Password health" (health_screen_title above). -->
    <string name="health_score_accessibility">Password health score %1$d out of 100</string>

    <string name="health_always_on_heading">Always on</string>
    <string name="health_always_on_footer">None of this is part of Pro, and none of it can be switched off.</string>
    <!-- Accessibility label for one "always on" protection row: title, then summary, then the fixed claim. -->
    <string name="health_always_on_item_accessibility">%1$s. %2$s. Always on.</string>

    <string name="health_scanning_title">Scanning your vault on this device</string>
    <!-- Accessibility label read instead of health_scanning_title while a scan is in progress. -->
    <string name="health_scanning_accessibility">Scanning your vault. This runs entirely on this device.</string>

    <!-- Score card verdict line: no findings at all yet because the vault is empty. -->
    <string name="health_verdict_nothing_to_check">Nothing to check yet</string>
    <!-- Score card verdict line: scanned, and nothing in any category. -->
    <string name="health_verdict_nothing_worth_fixing">Nothing worth fixing</string>
    <!-- Score card verdict line: no password faults, only duplicate entries remain. -->
    <string name="health_verdict_healthy_duplicates">Healthy — only duplicate entries left</string>
    <!-- Score card verdict line: no password faults, only ageing passwords remain. -->
    <string name="health_verdict_healthy_ageing">Healthy — only ageing passwords left</string>
    <!-- Score card verdict line when one or more items have a password fault (breached, reused,
         or weak, counted once each). %1$d is affectedCount, substituted only in the "other" form. -->
    <plurals name="health_verdict_affected_count">
        <item quantity="one">Good, with one thing worth fixing</item>
        <item quantity="other">Good, with %1$d things worth fixing</item>
    </plurals>
    <!-- The same line when the score falls short of GOOD_VERDICT_MIN_SCORE or any password matched the breach set, where
         "Good" would contradict the ring and the advice under it. Same quantity as above. -->
    <plurals name="health_verdict_affected_count_attention">
        <item quantity="one">Needs attention, with one thing worth fixing</item>
        <item quantity="other">Needs attention, with %1$d things worth fixing</item>
    </plurals>

    <!-- Advice line: empty vault, nothing yet to analyse. -->
    <string name="health_advice_empty">Add some logins and this screen will have something to say.</string>
    <!-- advice line: at least one item matched the offline breach set.
         Highest priority.

         Said "it is already in a public breach set" until 2026-08-31, flatly and with no hedge,
         which is a stronger claim than the screen's own category label ("Potentially compromised"
         / "Matched the offline breach set") and stronger than the filter can make: a Golomb-Rice
         set has a disclosed false-positive rate, so a match is a match against a compressed
         summary of the list, not membership of it. It now names the match and the action it
         should trigger, and keeps the "offline" scope word for the same reason
         health_advice_clean_breach_checked does — an offline app cannot speak for every breach
         that exists. -->
    <!-- SECURITY-COPY: tier G -->
    <plurals name="health_advice_breached">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d password matched the offline breach set — start there, and treat it as compromised.</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d passwords matched the offline breach set — start there, and treat them as compromised.</item>
    </plurals>
    <!-- Advice line: no breach hits, but a password is reused across items. -->
    <string name="health_advice_reused">Start with the reused passwords — one breach there exposes every account sharing it.</string>
    <!-- Advice line: no breach or reuse hits, but a password is weak. -->
    <string name="health_advice_weak">Start with the weak passwords — the generator will replace one in a couple of taps.</string>
    <!-- Advice line: no password faults, only ageing passwords to rotate. -->
    <string name="health_advice_old">Nothing is broken. Rotate the oldest few when you next have a spare minute.</string>
    <!-- Advice line: no password faults or ageing passwords, only duplicate entries. -->
    <string name="health_advice_duplicate">The passwords are fine. Some accounts are saved twice — worth merging, or the next password change will only take on one copy.</string>
    <!-- Advice line: a completely clean scan that did consult the offline breach set. States the
         breach-check result explicitly — see health_advice_clean_no_breach_data for the case where
         the corpus never loaded and this claim would not be true. -->
    <!-- SECURITY-COPY: tier G -->
    <string name="health_advice_clean_breach_checked">Every password here is unique, not weak, and not in the offline breach set.</string>
    <!-- Advice line: a completely clean scan where the breach corpus was unavailable, so nothing
         is claimed about breach status (see HealthAdviceBreachClaimTest). -->
    <string name="health_advice_clean_no_breach_data">Every password here is unique and not weak.</string>

    <!-- Error card shown when the scan coroutine fails, most commonly an auto-lock landing mid-scan. -->
    <string name="health_error_scan_failed">The security scan could not finish. Your vault may have locked while it was running — open it again and retry.</string>
    <!-- The second line of a category row whose entry has no username (a phrase, a note), so two
         copies of one title still differ. %1$s is the locale's medium date. -->
    <string name="health_row_saved_on">Saved %1$s</string>
    <!-- Shown under the Health verdict when entries are concealed in this session, so a perfect
         score is not read as a statement about the whole vault. -->
    <plurals name="health_excluded_note">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d invisible entry was not checked.</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d invisible entries were not checked.</item>
    </plurals>
</resources>
